<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger I of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1031–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Roger de Hauteville, Great Count Roger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger I was a Norman adventurer of the Hauteville family, youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from its Muslim rulers. He crossed the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 1061, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> with Robert in 1072 and completed the conquest in 1091, ruling as Count of Sicily. He governed a mixed Greek, Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> population with tolerance, and his son Roger II became the first King of Sicily.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Roger I of Sicily" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calabria,_trifollaro_di_ruggieri_I_d%27altavilla,_1072-1101.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century Norman adventurers led by the Hauteville family took Apulia and Calabria from the Byzantines and Lombard princes, and between 1061 and 1091 Count <b>Roger I</b> conquered Sicily from its Muslim emirs, taking Palermo in 1072 together with his brother Robert Guiscard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roger de Hauteville</b>, Robert Guiscard's youngest brother, crossed to Sicily in 1061 and took Messina.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sicily">County of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
